var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Using-it-in-LaTeX","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"","title":"Using it in LaTeX"},{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Preamble","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{lmodern}   % or any scalable font\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}  % only if figures contain heatmaps, images or rasterised plots\n\nWith the default fontsize = :native, text nodes request exact sizes such as \\fontsize{10.5}{12.6}, so use a scalable font: lmodern, fix-cm, or any OpenType font under LuaLaTeX/XeLaTeX. Plain OT1 Computer Modern substitutes the nearest size and warns. With LuaLaTeX/XeLaTeX and a Unicode font, pass unicode = false to keep characters such as −, ° and Greek letters as they are.","title":"Preamble"},{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Matching-the-document","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"LaTeX typesets the text, but Makie's layout decided where it goes and how much room it gets. For the best match, build the figure at its final size and font size, and let Makie measure text with Computer Modern:\n\nwith_theme(latex_theme(fontsize = 10)) do      # 10pt text, Computer Modern metrics\n    fig = Figure(size = size_pt(345, 200))     # 345pt = \\textwidth of a 10pt article\n    ...\n    savetikz(\"fig.tex\", fig)\nend\n\nsize_pt converts TeX points to Makie units (1 unit = 0.75pt by default, as in CairoMakie's PDFs).\nlatex_theme sets Makie's font size so text comes out at the requested size, and switches Makie to Computer Modern so its layout measures text the way LaTeX will set it.\nThe whole picture can be rescaled in LaTeX by editing x=0.75pt, y=0.75pt in the tikzpicture options. Line widths and text keep their absolute size.\n\nTo find your document's text width, put \\showthe\\textwidth in it and look in the log.","title":"Matching the document"},{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Images-and-file-layout","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"Heatmaps, images and rasterised plots are written as PNGs next to the .tex file (plot.tex → plot-1.png, …). \\includegraphics resolves paths relative to the main document, so if you \\input{figures/plot.tex} either pass image_prefix = \"figures/\" to savetikz or add \\graphicspath{{figures/}} to the preamble.","title":"Images and file layout"},{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Rasterising-heavy-plots","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"Set Makie's own rasterize attribute on a plot to embed it as an image while everything else stays vector and LaTeX. This needs CairoMakie loaded:\n\nusing CairoMakie, TikzMakie\nscatter!(ax, huge_points; rasterize = true)   # rasterize_scale px per unit (default 4)\nsurface!(ax3, xs, ys, zs; rasterize = 6)      # or an explicit px per unit","title":"Rasterising heavy plots"},{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Tables-and-other-raw-LaTeX","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"Makie cannot draw a tabular (its LaTeX renderer only does math), but TikzMakie can pass one through. RawLaTeX is text that goes into the picture verbatim, while Makie's layout and other backends see a plain-text preview. It works anywhere Makie takes text: text!, Label, titles.\n\nlatextable builds one from any table with PrettyTables.jl. The LaTeX comes from PrettyTables' LaTeX backend (booktabs rules by default) and the preview from its text backend, so Makie reserves roughly the space the table will take:\n\nusing CairoMakie, TikzMakie, PrettyTables\n\ndata = [1 0.12 \"a\"; 2 3.4 \"b\"; 3 56.7 \"c\"]\nlabels = [LatexCell(L\"n\"), LatexCell(L\"x\"), \"label\"]\n\nfig = Figure(size = size_pt(345, 150))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = L\"t\", ylabel = L\"\\sin t\")\nlines!(ax, 0 .. 10, sin)\n# an inset, on a white background so the data doesn't show through\ntext!(ax, 0.98, 0.97; text = latextable(data; column_labels = labels, background = \"white\"),\n      space = :relative, align = (:right, :top))\n# a table in its own layout cell\nLabel(fig[1, 2], latextable(data; column_labels = labels); tellheight = false)\n\nsavetikz(\"table.tex\", fig; standalone = true, preamble = \"\\\\usepackage{booktabs}\")\n\nThe table is set at the text's font size. In your own document, add \\usepackage{booktabs} to the preamble (or pass booktabs = false). Keywords meant for one PrettyTables backend only go in latex = (; ...) or text = (; ...).","title":"Tables and other raw LaTeX"},{"category":"section","location":"latex/#Options","page":"Using it in LaTeX","text":"All keywords of tikz/savetikz are listed in TikzOptions. The most useful:\n\noption default \npt_per_unit 0.75 TeX points per Makie unit.\nfontsize :native :native sets each node's size from Makie; :inherit uses the surrounding document's size.\nnumbers_as_math true Numeric strings (tick labels) are set in math mode: $-1.5$, $10^{-3}$.\ndisplaystyle false Prefix math with \\displaystyle, so \\frac/\\int are full size, as Makie draws them.\nunicode true Replace −, °, ×, Greek letters, … with LaTeX commands (for pdfLaTeX).\ntextfilter identity Function applied to each node's LaTeX string.\nbackground false Draw the figure background (false = transparent).\nstandalone false Wrap in a compilable standalone document.\npreamble \"\" Extra preamble lines for standalone, e.g. \"\\\\usepackage{booktabs}\".\nimage_prefix \"\" Prefix for \\includegraphics paths.\nsimplify 0.05 Line simplification tolerance in Makie units. 0 disables.\nrasterize_scale 4 Pixels per unit for plots with rasterize = true.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#TikzMakie.tikz","page":"API","text":"tikz(fig; kwargs...) -> TikzFigure\n\nConvert a Makie Figure, FigureAxisPlot or Scene to TikZ. See TikzOptions for the keyword arguments. The result's code is a tikzpicture environment (or a standalone document with standalone=true); raster images it references are in images and are written by savetikz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.tikz"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#TikzMakie.savetikz","page":"API","text":"savetikz(path, fig; kwargs...)\nsavetikz(path, t::TikzFigure)\n\nWrite the TikZ code for fig to path (usually .tex) and any images next to it as <name>-<n>.png. Include the result in a document with \\input{path}. Returns path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.savetikz"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#TikzMakie.TikzFigure","page":"API","text":"TikzFigure\n\nResult of tikz: the TikZ code (String(t) or t.code) plus any raster images (t.images, filename => pixels) that the code references. Save both with savetikz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.TikzFigure"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#TikzMakie.TikzOptions","page":"API","text":"Options controlling TikZ output. Every field can be passed as a keyword to tikz and savetikz.\n\npt_per_unit = 0.75: size of one Makie unit in TeX points. 0.75 matches CairoMakie's PDF output, so a 600×450 figure becomes 450pt×337.5pt.\ndigits = 2: decimals written for coordinates (in Makie units).\nfontsize = :native: :native sets each text node's size from Makie (\\fontsize), so the text fills the space Makie's layout reserved; :inherit leaves the document's font size alone.\nnumbers_as_math = true: typeset purely numeric strings (tick labels) in math mode, $-1.5$.\nunicode = true: replace common Unicode symbols (−, °, Greek, …) with LaTeX commands for pdfLaTeX. Set false for LuaLaTeX/XeLaTeX with a Unicode font.\ntextfilter = identity: function applied to the final LaTeX string of every text node, for custom substitutions.\nbackground = false: draw the figure's background colour. false leaves the picture transparent (axis backgrounds are still drawn).\nstandalone = false: wrap the picture in a compilable standalone document.\npreamble = \"\": extra preamble lines for the standalone document, e.g. \"\\\\usepackage{booktabs}\" for a latextable.\nimage_prefix = \"\": prepended to image file names in \\includegraphics, e.g. \"figures/\" when the .tex is \\input from a parent directory.\nimage_basename = \"tikzmakie\": stem of image file names (set automatically by savetikz).\nmax_upsample = 2048: non-interpolated images (heatmaps) are upsampled with nearest-neighbour to at most this many pixels per side, so PDF viewers cannot blur them.\ncolor_prefix = \"mkc\": prefix for \\definecolor names.\nclip_padding = 0.25: geometry further than this fraction of the figure size outside the figure is cut in Julia (TikZ cannot handle huge coordinates).\nsimplify = 0.05: lines are simplified (Ramer–Douglas–Peucker) with this tolerance in Makie units (0.05 units ≈ 0.04pt, far below visibility). Dense lines shrink a lot. 0 disables.\nrasterize_scale = 4: pixels per Makie unit for plots with rasterize = true (≈ 380 dpi at the default pt_per_unit). An integer rasterize = n ≥ 2 on the plot uses n instead (Makie stores true as 1). Needs CairoMakie loaded.\nstrut = true: give every text node at least the height and depth of a parenthesis, as Makie aligns text by font ascender/descender rather than by the glyphs' ink. Keeps e.g. an x axis label at the same distance from the tick labels as in Makie.\ndisplaystyle = false: prefix math in LaTeXStrings with \\displaystyle, so \\frac, \\sum and \\int are full size, as Makie draws them (and as Makie's layout reserved space for them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.TikzOptions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#TikzMakie.latex_theme","page":"API","text":"latex_theme(; fontsize = 10, pt_per_unit = 0.75, latexfonts = true)\n\nA Makie theme for figures destined for LaTeX: text of fontsize points once written with savetikz at pt_per_unit, and (with latexfonts = true) Computer Modern fonts so that Makie's layout measures text close to how LaTeX will typeset it.\n\nwith_theme(latex_theme(fontsize = 10)) do\n    fig = Figure(size = size_pt(345, 220))\n    ...\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.latex_theme"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#TikzMakie.size_pt","page":"API","text":"size_pt(width, height; pt_per_unit = 0.75)\n\nFigure size in Makie units for a picture of width×height TeX points. For an article's \\textwidth (345pt): Figure(size = size_pt(345, 220)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.size_pt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#TikzMakie.RawLaTeX","page":"API","text":"RawLaTeX(latex; preview = latex)\n\nText that TikzMakie writes into the picture verbatim, as LaTeX source, while Makie (its layout, CairoMakie, GLMakie, …) sees only preview. Use it for LaTeX that Makie cannot draw itself, such as a tabular:\n\ntbl = raw\"\\begin{tabular}{rr} \\toprule a & b \\\\ \\midrule 1 & 2 \\\\ \\bottomrule \\end{tabular}\"\ntext!(ax, 0.95, 0.95; text = RawLaTeX(tbl; preview = \"a  b\\n1  2\"),\n      space = :relative, align = (:right, :top))\nLabel(fig[1, 2], RawLaTeX(tbl; preview = \"a  b\\n1  2\"))\n\nRawLaTeX is an AbstractString that behaves like preview, so it works anywhere Makie takes text. Makie reserves space for the preview, so make the preview roughly the size of the typeset result. The node keeps its font size, colour, alignment and rotation; the LaTeX is not escaped and its line breaks are not treated as line breaks.\n\nlatextable builds one from a table with PrettyTables.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.RawLaTeX"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#TikzMakie.latextable","page":"API","text":"latextable(data; booktabs = true, background = nothing, latex = (;), text = (;), kwargs...) -> RawLaTeX\n\nA table for a Makie figure, typeset by LaTeX in TikzMakie's output. Needs PrettyTables.jl loaded (using PrettyTables). data is anything pretty_table accepts (a matrix, a vector, a Tables.jl table).\n\nThe LaTeX comes from pretty_table(String, data; backend = :latex, ...), and the RawLaTeX preview, which Makie draws and sizes, from the :text backend. Keywords:\n\nkwargs...: passed to both backends (column_labels, formatters, alignment, …). LatexCells are shown in the preview without $, {} and backslashes.\nlatex, text: named tuples of keywords for one backend only.\nbooktabs = true: \\toprule/\\midrule/\\bottomrule (needs \\usepackage{booktabs}) unless latex sets table_format.\nbackground: an xcolor name such as \"white\", to set the table on an opaque \\colorbox (useful for insets over data).\n\nusing CairoMakie, TikzMakie, PrettyTables\ntbl = latextable([1 0.12; 2 3.4]; column_labels = [LatexCell(L\"n\"), LatexCell(L\"x\")])\ntext!(ax, 0.98, 0.97; text = tbl, space = :relative, align = (:right, :top))\nLabel(fig[1, 2], tbl; tellheight = false)\nsavetikz(\"fig.tex\", fig; standalone = true, preamble = \"\\\\usepackage{booktabs}\")\n\nThe preview uses the text's font, so its columns only line up in a monospace font (font = \"DejaVu Sans Mono\" if installed); the size Makie reserves is approximate either way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"TikzMakie.latextable"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Gallery","page":"Gallery","text":"Each figure below is the output of savetikz compiled with pdfLaTeX (left), next to CairoMakie's rendering of the same figure (right). Text in the TikZ version is typeset by LaTeX, here in Latin Modern. In your document it will use your fonts. Every example starts with using CairoMakie, TikzMakie, LaTeXStrings and ends with savetikz(\"name.tex\", fig); the .tex links are exactly what that writes.","title":"Gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Basic-line-plot","page":"Gallery","text":"Tick labels, axis labels, legend and title are all LaTeX text nodes; L\"...\" strings become real LaTeX math.\n\nxs = range(0, 4π, length = 200)\nfig = Figure(size = (600, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = L\"t\", ylabel = L\"f(t)\", title = \"Two signals\")\nlines!(ax, xs, sin.(xs); label = L\"\\sin t\")\nlines!(ax, xs, cos.(xs) .* exp.(-xs ./ 8); label = L\"e^{-t/8}\\cos t\", linestyle = :dash, linewidth = 2)\naxislegend(ax; position = :rt)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 01_basic.tex · PDF","title":"Basic line plot"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Sized-for-a-document","page":"Gallery","text":"size_pt and latex_theme make the picture exactly \\textwidth wide with 10pt Computer Modern text.\n\nwith_theme(latex_theme(fontsize = 10)) do\n    fig = Figure(size = size_pt(345, 200))   # 345pt = \\textwidth of a 10pt article\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = L\"time $t$ (s)\", ylabel = L\"x(t)\", title = \"Damped oscillator\")\n    ts = range(0, 10, length = 300)\n    for γ in (0.1, 0.3, 1.0)\n        lines!(ax, ts, exp.(-γ .* ts) .* cos.(2ts); label = L\"\\gamma = %$γ\")\n    end\n    axislegend(ax; position = :rt)\n    ax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"Phase portrait\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"\\dot{x}\", aspect = 1)\n    x = exp.(-0.1 .* ts) .* cos.(2ts)\n    v = -0.1 .* x .- 2 .* exp.(-0.1 .* ts) .* sin.(2ts)\n    lines!(ax2, x, v; color = ts, colormap = :viridis)\n    colsize!(fig.layout, 2, Auto(0.6))\n    fig\nend\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 02_article_width.tex · PDF","title":"Sized for a document"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Scatter-markers","page":"Gallery","text":"Circles, rectangles and Makie's Bézier markers become TikZ paths; colormapped colours and sizes are kept.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (600, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"Marker shapes\", yticks = (1:7, [\"circle\", \"rect\", \"utriangle\", \"diamond\", \"cross\", \"star5\", \"hexagon\"]))\nfor (i, m) in enumerate([:circle, :rect, :utriangle, :diamond, :cross, :star5, :hexagon])\n    scatter!(ax, 1:6, fill(i, 6); marker = m, markersize = 16, strokewidth = 1, strokecolor = :black)\nend\nscatter!(ax, 1:6, fill(8, 6); color = 1:6, colormap = :plasma, markersize = 6:5:31)\nylims!(ax, 0.3, 8.9)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 03_markers.tex · PDF","title":"Scatter markers"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Text,-math-and-escaping","page":"Gallery","text":"LaTeXStrings pass through unchanged, rich text maps to super/subscripts, and plain strings are escaped.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (600, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = L\"\\alpha \\in [0, 2\\pi]\", ylabel = L\"\\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\alpha}\", title = L\"\\mathrm{sinc}(\\alpha) = \\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\alpha}\")\nxs = range(0.01, 2π, length = 200)\nlines!(ax, xs, sin.(xs) ./ xs)\ntext!(ax, 3.2, 0.7; text = \"plain text: 50% & #1_x {braces} ~ ^\", align = (:center, :center))\ntext!(ax, 1.0, -0.05; text = rich(\"rich: x\", superscript(\"2\"), \" + y\", subscript(\"i\")), rotation = π / 8)\ntext!(ax, 4.5, 0.25; text = L\"\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}\", align = (:center, :center))\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 04_text.tex · PDF","title":"Text, math and escaping"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Bars-and-histograms","page":"Gallery","text":"Polygons are written as single vector paths, not triangle meshes, so there are no hairline seams.\n\nusing Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\nfig = Figure(size = (650, 300))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"barplot\", xticks = (1:4, [\"north\", \"east\", \"south\", \"west\"]))\nbarplot!(ax1, 1:4, [3.2, 1.4, 4.1, 2.6]; color = [:steelblue, :orange, :seagreen, :crimson], bar_labels = :y, strokewidth = 1)\nylims!(ax1, 0, 5)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"hist\")\nhist!(ax2, randn(2000); bins = 30, strokewidth = 0.5, strokecolor = :white)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 05_bars_hist.tex · PDF","title":"Bars and histograms"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Heatmap-and-colorbar","page":"Gallery","text":"Regular heatmaps and images are embedded as PNGs (upsampled so viewers don't blur them).\n\nxs = range(-3, 3, length = 40)\nzs = [sin(x) * cos(y) + 0.3x for x in xs, y in xs]\nfig = Figure(size = (560, 420))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"y\", title = L\"z = \\sin x \\cos y + 0.3x\", aspect = DataAspect())\nhm = heatmap!(ax, xs, xs, zs; colormap = :balance)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm; label = L\"z\")\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 06_heatmap.tex · PDF","title":"Heatmap and colorbar"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Filled-contours","page":"Gallery","text":"xs = range(-2, 2, length = 80)\nf(x, y) = exp(-(x^2 + y^2)) + 0.6exp(-((x - 1)^2 + (y - 1)^2) / 0.3)\nzs = [f(x, y) for x in xs, y in xs]\nfig = Figure(size = (560, 440))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"contourf + contour\", aspect = DataAspect())\ncf = contourf!(ax, xs, xs, zs; levels = 10)\ncontour!(ax, xs, xs, zs; levels = 10, color = :black, linewidth = 0.5)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], cf)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 07_contour.tex · PDF","title":"Filled contours"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Layouts","page":"Gallery","text":"GridLayout positions, spanning labels, rotated tick labels and shared legends carry over unchanged.\n\nusing Random\nRandom.seed!(2)\nfig = Figure(size = (650, 480))\nLabel(fig[0, 1:2], \"Four panels\"; fontsize = 20, font = :bold)\nfor (k, (i, j)) in enumerate(Iterators.product(1:2, 1:2))\n    ax = Axis(fig[i, j]; title = \"panel $k\", xticklabelrotation = i == 2 ? π / 4 : 0.0)\n    for s in 1:3\n        lines!(ax, 1:20, cumsum(randn(20)); label = \"series $s\")\n    end\n    hlines!(ax, [0]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dot)\nend\nLegend(fig[1:2, 3], content(fig[1, 1]); framevisible = false)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 08_layout.tex · PDF","title":"Layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Statistical-plots","page":"Gallery","text":"using Random\nRandom.seed!(3)\nfig = Figure(size = (650, 450))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"boxplot + violin\", xticks = (1:3, [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"]))\ncats = rand(1:3, 300)\nvals = randn(300) .+ 0.5cats\nviolin!(ax1, cats, vals; color = (:steelblue, 0.4))\nboxplot!(ax1, cats, vals; width = 0.3, color = :white, strokewidth = 1, whiskerwidth = 0.3)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; title = \"errorbars + band\")\nxs = 1:10\nys = sqrt.(xs)\nband!(ax2, xs, ys .- 0.4, ys .+ 0.4; color = (:orange, 0.3))\nerrorbars!(ax2, xs, ys, 0.2 .* ys; whiskerwidth = 6)\nscatter!(ax2, xs, ys; color = :black)\nax3 = Axis(fig[2, 1:2]; title = \"density + stairs + stem\")\ndensity!(ax3, randn(500); color = (:seagreen, 0.4), strokewidth = 1, strokecolor = :seagreen)\nstairs!(ax3, -3:0.5:3, rand(13) .* 0.4; color = :crimson)\nstem!(ax3, -3:0.5:3, rand(13) .* -0.2)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 09_statistics.tex · PDF","title":"Statistical plots"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Log-axes","page":"Gallery","text":"Makie's superscript tick labels become math: $10^{-4}$.\n\nns = 2 .^ (1:12)\nfig = Figure(size = (560, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xscale = log2, yscale = log10, xlabel = L\"N\", ylabel = \"error\", title = \"Convergence\")\nscatterlines!(ax, ns, 1 ./ ns; label = L\"\\mathcal{O}(N^{-1})\")\nscatterlines!(ax, ns, 1 ./ ns .^ 2; label = L\"\\mathcal{O}(N^{-2})\", marker = :rect)\naxislegend(ax; position = :lb)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 10_log_axes.tex · PDF","title":"Log axes"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#3D-and-rasterisation","page":"Gallery","text":"Vector 3D meshes are flat-shaded; set Makie's rasterize attribute to embed a lit surface as an image while axes and labels stay vector + LaTeX.\n\nxs = range(-1, 1, length = 40)\nzs = [x^2 - y^2 for x in xs, y in xs]\nfig = Figure(size = (700, 330))\nax1 = Axis3(fig[1, 1]; title = \"vector (flat-shaded)\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"y\", zlabel = L\"z\")\nsurface!(ax1, xs[1:3:end], xs[1:3:end], zs[1:3:end, 1:3:end])\nax2 = Axis3(fig[1, 2]; title = \"rasterize = true\", xlabel = L\"x\", ylabel = L\"y\", zlabel = L\"z\")\nsurface!(ax2, xs, xs, zs; rasterize = true)\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 11_axis3_rasterize.tex · PDF","title":"3D and rasterisation"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Polar-axis","page":"Gallery","text":"θ = range(0, 2π, length = 400)\nfig = Figure(size = (450, 450))\nax = PolarAxis(fig[1, 1]; title = \"Rose curves\")\nfor k in (2, 3, 5)\n    lines!(ax, θ, abs.(cos.(k .* θ)); label = \"k = $k\")\nend\nfig\n\nTikzMakie → pdfLaTeX CairoMakie\n(Image: TikZ output) (Image: CairoMakie output)\n\nDownload: 12_polar.tex · PDF","title":"Polar axis"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#How-it-works","page":"How it works","text":"TikzMakie walks the figure's scene tree the way CairoMakie does, but writes TikZ instead of making Cairo calls:\n\nSettle the layout (Makie.update_state_before_display!).\nFill scene backgrounds.\nCollect the primitive plots (lines, scatter, text, meshes, images, …). Poly and Band are kept whole so they become clean vector paths rather than triangle meshes, and Text is kept whole so it becomes LaTeX nodes.\nSort by depth, and open a \\clip scope for each scene viewport.\nProject every position through Makie's own camera matrices to figure coordinates (Makie units, y up, the same orientation as TikZ), and write TikZ.\n\nText is read from the plot before Makie lays out glyphs: the original string, its alignment (mapped to a TikZ anchor), rotation, size, colour and font weight. Makie aligns text by the font's ascender/descender, so every node gets a \\vphantom{(} strut to mimic that.\n\nGeometry far outside the figure is clipped in Julia (Liang–Barsky for lines, Sutherland–Hodgman for fills), because TikZ cannot handle huge coordinates. Dense lines are simplified with Ramer–Douglas–Peucker at a tolerance far below visibility.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Limitations","page":"How it works","text":"Text width: LaTeX typesets the text after Makie has laid out the figure. With latex_theme the widths agree closely; with very different fonts, long labels may not fit the space Makie reserved.\n3D meshes are flat-shaded and intersecting triangles are not split. Use rasterize.\nPattern fills become grey; character markers lose their outline.\nUses Makie internals (the 0.24 compute graph), so each Makie minor release may need an update.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#TikzMakie.jl","page":"Home","text":"Turn Makie figures into TikZ code for LaTeX documents, with all text typeset by LaTeX: tick labels, axis labels, legends and titles appear in your document's font, and L\"...\" strings become real LaTeX math.\n\nusing CairoMakie, TikzMakie   # any Makie backend, or none at all\n\nfig = lines(0..10, sin; axis = (; xlabel = L\"t\", ylabel = L\"\\sin(t)\"))\nsavetikz(\"sine.tex\", fig)\n\n\\usepackage{tikz}       % plus graphicx if the figure contains images/heatmaps\n...\n\\begin{figure}\n  \\centering\n  \\input{sine.tex}\n\\end{figure}\n\nNothing about your plotting code changes. TikzMakie reads the finished figure, so the output has the same layout as CairoMakie's PDF. See the Gallery for side-by-side comparisons.","title":"TikzMakie.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/EvoArt/TikzMakie.jl\")\n\nRequires Makie 0.24 and Julia ≥ 1.10.","title":"Installation"}]
}
